chx:F1 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Chapter 20 of the 3rd edition, Sending Emails, Texts, and Push Notifications, teaches three ways for a program to reach a person: the EZGmail module over the Gmail API, which sends mail with send() (attachments, cc and bcc), reads it as conversation threads and messages with unread(), recent() and summary(), searches with Gmail's search operators and downloads attachments; texts sent as email to a carrier's SMS email gateway, with a table of twelve providers and five disadvantages; and push notifications through the free ntfy service, sent with requests.post() and a topic name, extended with a title, a priority from 1 to 5 and tags as headers, and received by polling /json?poll=1 and parsing one JSON object per line; it closes with 5 practice questions and 3 practice programs (Umbrella Reminder, Auto Unsubscriber, Email-Based Computer Control)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01 .
chx:F2 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter says that Gmail's security measures make the EZGmail module a better route than the standard library's smtplib and imaplib; the documentation of Google agrees in part and adds what the chapter omits: Gmail accepts standard IMAP, POP and SMTP at imap.gmail.com on port 993 and smtp.gmail.com on port 465 or 587 when the client authorizes with OAuth 2.0, and since 14 March 2025 a third-party client can no longer use a plain account password there, the exception being an app password, which Google says is not recommended and needs 2-Step Verification; the standard library's smtplib and imaplib therefore remain usable for Gmail, but their login by password is what stopped working." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P03, chx:P24, chx:P25, chx:P26 .
chx:F3 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's description of EZGmail holds against version 2026.4.28 of the module as read in its source, with differences the chapter does not state: the attachments attribute of a message is a list of file names (as the chapter says), while the README's example of it prints a dictionary with id and size; a message timestamp is made with datetime.fromtimestamp from the internal date in milliseconds without a time zone, so it shows the clock time of the computer that runs the program; summary() takes each sender's first name as the text up to the first space, so a sender text without a space loses its last character; downloadAllAttachments() has an overwrite argument that defaults to True, which matches the chapter's statement that existing files are overwritten; the README lists cc and bcc both in its quick start and among the limitations, and the source accepts both; and the module builds its message with the legacy email.mime classes." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P04, chx:P05, chx:P11, chx:P21, chx:P22 .
chx:F4 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's printed outputs of the ntfy section reproduce in kind under Python 3.14.4 on a local stand-in for the service: a POST with a body is a request with a method of POST, a GET without a body is a GET, the headers Title, Tags and Priority are carried as written, the response text of a poll holds one JSON object per line and json.loads() on the whole text raises JSONDecodeError 'Extra data: line 2 column 1 (char 134)' for the chapter's two lines, and each line parsed alone gives the dictionaries the chapter shows; the one printed value that depends on the machine is the chapter's str(datetime.datetime.fromtimestamp(1797823712)), which is '2026-12-20 21:28:32' at six hours behind UTC and '2026-12-21 03:28:32' at UTC; the service itself was not contacted." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P08, chx:P10, chx:P11, chx:P19, chx:P20 .
chx:F5 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's statements about ntfy agree with its documentation, which also says more: a message may be 4,096 bytes and longer text is treated as an attachment, the free service's daily limit is 250 messages, priority 1 to 5 has the names min, low, default, high and max, since accepts a duration, a Unix timestamp, a message id, all or latest, the event key has the values open, keepalive, message, message_delete, message_clear and poll_request where the chapter lists four, a poll without since returns the topic's whole cache with replay limits, the documentation advises passing the id of the last message as since, and a header value that is not plain ASCII should be sent as an RFC 2047 encoded word; the combined duration 2h30m that the chapter gives for since is not among the examples in the pages read." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P19, chx:P20 .
chx:F6 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's table of SMS email gateways is a snapshot that has aged: vendor notices report that AT&T ended its email-to-text gateway on 17 June 2025, so the two AT&T addresses of the table no longer deliver, that Cricket, an AT&T subsidiary, is affected as well, and that other carriers reduced support or delivery guarantees; these are vendor statements and not the carriers' own, and the table's other addresses were not tested, since sending a text needs a phone number and a live gateway; the chapter's five disadvantages of a gateway are consistent with what the vendors describe." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P28, chx:P29 .
chx:F7 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Current Python gives the chapter's topics more than it presents: email.message.EmailMessage, whose policy is the current one, builds and reads a message, adds attachments with add_attachment() and lists them with iter_attachments(), while the email.mime classes that EZGmail uses belong to the legacy compat32 interface; smtplib's send_message() reads the recipients from To, Cc and Bcc and does not transmit the Bcc field; email.utils.parseaddr() and getaddresses() take apart the sender and address lists; the module smtpd was removed in Python 3.12, so the standard library has no test SMTP server, which this study replaced by a short socketserver handler on the loopback address; the secrets module, not random, is the source for a secret topic name; and html.parser can do the link finding that the Auto Unsubscriber asks of Beautiful Soup." ; sen0414:cites chx:P02, chx:P04, chx:P05, chx:P06, chx:P14, chx:P15, chx:P30 .
chx:F8 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Modern practice warns of traps the chapter states briefly or not at all: a message limit counts bytes, so text with letters such as ı or ş is longer in bytes than in characters; HTTP libraries send a header value as Latin-1, so a title with a letter outside it raises UnicodeEncodeError unless it is sent as an encoded word; a topic name is the only protection of a notification, so it is chosen with secrets, compared case-sensitively and never used for passwords or card numbers; a command channel needs a check that does not rest on the From address and a fixed list of allowed commands, for which hmac.compare_digest() compares a password in constant time; and every keyed lookup into a notification needs get(), because title, priority and tags are present only when set." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P12, chx:P15, chx:P16, chx:P19, chx:P20 .
chx:F9 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Conclusion"@en ; sen0414:findingText "For an advanced course, chapter 20 is best taught as three channels judged by the same questions: what reaches the person, what proves the sender and what can be tested without sending; the parts that can be executed offline are the message objects of the standard library, the address and query parsing, the base64 and JSON handling, the request objects of urllib and the parsing of a poll response, and all of them reproduce under Python 3.14.4, while the sending through Gmail, the reading of a real mailbox, a text through a carrier and a call to ntfy.sh were read from the book and its sources and not run, because each needs an account, a phone or the network; the chapter's Gmail claim needs the update about OAuth and app passwords, its gateway table needs the AT&T correction, and its ntfy and EZGmail statements hold with the additions of findings 3, 4 and 5." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P19, chx:P20, chx:P21, chx:P22, chx:P25, chx:P28 .
chx:Scorecard a sen0414:QualityScorecard ; rdfs:label "Quality scorecard"@en ; sen0414:topicCoverage "35 primary-source concepts inventoried from the textbook ontology." ; sen0414:citationStrength "30 sources, all opened and verified at 2026-10-01T12:00:00; 23 cited by findings." ; sen0414:methodologyCompliance "RDODI procedure v1.6.0 Stage 1." ; sen0414:reproducibility "Every source is a stable public URL; every claim names its sources." .
